WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.996 --> 00:00:04.482
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.956
[WHISTLES]

00:00:13.642 --> 00:00:14.442
Hey.

00:00:21.912 --> 00:00:22.908
Oh, yeah.

00:00:25.896 --> 00:00:27.390
Yes, thank you.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:34.336
Where's the paper?

00:00:34.336 --> 00:00:35.170
Dammit.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:38.210
We discussed this.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.294
It's time to get black, y'all.

00:00:41.294 --> 00:00:44.766
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:15.022
Your attention, please.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:18.960
Good day.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.040
Good evening.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.260
Good life.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.580
It's me, Craig
Robinson, and I'm here

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.940
to be your guide through a
journey of epic blackness.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.970
No, I'm not talking about my
skin, which is pretty epic.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.680
I'm talking about a new
batch of heroes that

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.650
are out in the world taking
charge in spaces and places

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.510
you will never imagine.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:41.280
I'm talking about coders, CEOs,
plant whisperers, just to name

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.720
a few.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.190
I'm going to be sort
of like your conduit

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.540
into these amazing stories.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.940
And hopefully, they inspire
y'all to go out there

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.870
and conquer your own calling,
even if it is something that

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.800
would be hard to explain
to your uncle at the family

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.450
reunion this year.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.170
So sit back, relax, and
let's enjoy together.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.990
Thank you.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.690
Say what?

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.526
I'm not done yet?

00:02:07.526 --> 00:02:10.030
Oh, I have to introduce the
first piece before I enjoy

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.640
this delicious truffle popcorn?

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.801
OK, gotcha.

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:17.070
Thank you.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:20.410
What do Ghana, Google, and
unicorns have in common?

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.000
They are all a part of the
epic journey of Iddris Sandu.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.550
Dude is a self-taught
computer wunderkind.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.920
He started coding at 13.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.670
When I was 13, the
only decoding I did

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.780
was looking for that ring at
the bottom of the cereal box.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.150
You see, Iddris is
literally out to change

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.500
the world through tech.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.670
And I'm definitely
not going to be

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:39.840
the one to tell him he can't.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.940
I heard he likes "Iron Man 2."

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.430
Maybe he can help me make
one of those chest thingies

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.360
so I can dominate
my spin classes.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.790
On that note, let's see
what Ghana, and Google,

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.275
and unicorns have in common.

00:02:52.275 --> 00:02:53.250
Wait, hold up.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.568
Ghana, and Google, and unicorn.

00:02:56.568 --> 00:02:59.038
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:59.038 --> 00:03:00.520
Your attention, please.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.780
Growing up, you know, we didn't
really have a lot of resources.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.590
We were a very low-income
family, and my mom raising me.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.340
And she would get these remote
controls from Food 4 Less,

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.120
or she would get them from,
you know, 99 cent stores.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.380
I remember she used
to spank me all

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.720
the time, 'cause I would, like,
break the remote control she

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.100
would buy.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.810
I would just always
look at the way

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.630
that circuit boards were made.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.980
And the first thing
that I ever programmed

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.690
was turning a light on.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.240
It was a diode, a circuit.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.660
It had a transistor
connected to it.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.940
And it would just literally
send a zero or one state.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:37.780
Zero is off.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:38.680
One is on.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.300
And that's when I
was really like,

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.100
I can input a value into
an equation, essentially,

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.860
and the output of it can have
an effect on the real world.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.050
From a young age, I just
understood that, no matter

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:51.970
what products we
ended up creating,

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:56.320
there was a story before
the product was created.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.560
Throughout my life, there's
been people that have been very

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.300
important in shaping my story.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.950
And one of those was my Google
colleague who discovered me

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.260
in a library at 13
years old and showed me

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.210
that I could elevate my skills.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:13.060
The backstory of it
is, I love the library,

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.860
and I was going to
the library every day.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.025
That was my virtual reality.

00:04:16.025 --> 00:04:17.769
It was like a safe haven for me.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:20.589
There, I could learn
the C#s, and the C++s,

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:25.690
and the Objective-C, Java,
Fortran, Java, .NET, 1.0, 2.0.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:28.630
One day, this Google designer
just happens to come in

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.983
and says he recalls
seeing this book online,

00:04:30.983 --> 00:04:31.950
and he wants to check it out.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.520
And the librarian is
like, the book should

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:34.930
be in the computer section.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:36.250
He goes to the computer section.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.150
The book isn't there.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:37.960
He comes back.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.907
She sends one of her
colleagues with him.

00:04:39.907 --> 00:04:40.840
They couldn't find the book.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.340
They both returned to the front.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.980
And it dawned on her that,
there is this kid that

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.420
comes in here every single day.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.290
And he goes to the same section,
and just reads these books

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:51.400
and leaves.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.130
In fact, I think I
saw him here today.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.380
And so she basically
points at the table I'm at.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:59.290
He walks up to me, and he's
like, if I borrow that book,

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:00.610
I promise I'll have it back.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.550
I was just like,
here, you can take it.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.800
And so he takes four steps
away, then turns, and is like,

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.990
do you know what you're reading?

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.120
And I was like, yeah.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.740
Like, I just want to
learn how to program.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.900
And as I start to
tell him who I am

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.120
and where I'm actually
from-- beyond just being

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.010
a kid from Compton that
moved to Harbor City,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.620
being a kid from
Ghana that came here

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.570
when he was three years
old, and how much I

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.550
wanted to use
technology to really

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.720
change lives and save lives.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.780
He offered me an
internship at Google,

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.270
and I was able to go there
for a couple of months.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:34.110
And being at one of the
largest companies in the world,

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:37.960
the insight that I received has
informed every single decision

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.310
I've ever made in my life.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.520
What that represented
to me was the ability

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.135
for somebody to really
believe in another person.

00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:49.450
And even from a young age, I
just knew that that was a gift.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.870
That was a chance that we speak
of-- being at the right place

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:54.610
at the right time.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:59.080
And in the same way as the
library, I was at a Starbucks

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.000
and just sat there
by the restroom,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.730
because it's the only section
facing the wall that no one

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.020
else can see what I'm doing.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.870
I was modifying software,
because I realized that I

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.360
wanted to create a
digital audio workstation

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.460
for kids in the hood to be able
to make music with their hands.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.180
And so I'm just, like, waving my
hands over my computer screen,

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:17.495
just doing this.

00:06:17.495 --> 00:06:20.810
I hear these chains
clacking against each other.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.630
I look up, and it
was Nipsey Hussle.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:25.330
And then he goes to the
front to order a unicorn

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.370
frappuccino for his daughter.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.140
I text one of my friends,
and I said, you know,

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:30.790
Nipsey also just walked in.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.890
I have a very strong
feeling that he's

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:33.760
going to come up to me.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.260
And then my friend
was like, Nipsey

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:36.580
ain't gonna come talk to you.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.200
So I'm literally
there, and I was like,

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:39.550
he's going to come talk to me.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:42.010
And that's the same feeling
that I felt the first time

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.240
of the Google designer.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.700
And he started walking my way.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.040
And I was like, I knew it.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.800
And then he walks right past
me and goes to the restroom.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.680
He's there for, like, 20
seconds and then comes right

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.390
out, and walks past me again.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:56.980
He did it four times.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.870
The fourth time, he
comes straight to me,

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.970
only to find out that
he didn't even need

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.710
to use the restroom at all.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:05.800
And in the most polite way that
anyone has ever approached me,

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:07.240
he says, excuse me, sir.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.040
I'm not trying to intrude
on what you're doing.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.580
But I know technology enough
that whatever you're doing

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.630
is not out.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.150
If you wouldn't mind me
asking, what are you doing?

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:19.580
I told him, and he just
saw it in that moment.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.790
And I remember
these exact words.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:25.330
He said, I want to put you in
a position where the same thing

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:28.090
you just told me, you can
tell that to everyone.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:29.740
I didn't know where
the dialogue would go.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.010
I didn't know if
he would hit me up.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:32.320
He hit me up that evening.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.950
The rest is history.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:38.200
It's really interesting how we
can draw these juxtapositions

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.240
between two separate worlds.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.400
One person that
helped me was white.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.510
The hood person that
helped me was black--

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.150
a yin and a yang.

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.680
But these two people had
the same amount of effect

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.525
and approached me the same way.

00:07:50.525 --> 00:07:52.060
And when we talk
about fulfillment,

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:56.530
every single person has at least
one time in their life where

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:59.170
you really reflect on
your life, and then you

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:00.970
say, what have I really done?

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:04.710
And I think I always knew that
my purpose, my mission, what

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:07.320
I would ultimately
leave this earth doing

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.840
is fulfilling something
greater than my own existence.

00:08:16.588 --> 00:08:19.970
Your attention, please.

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:22.170
The boy is bad.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.560
And that's bad meaning good, for
those who didn't get the slang

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:25.760
memo, like, 30 years ago.

00:08:32.059 --> 00:08:33.440
Moving right along.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:35.919
What does MAI stand for?

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:37.890
Hint, it's not what you think.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:40.350
And I definitely know
what you're thinking.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:44.360
MAI stands for Movement Art
Is, an organization that

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.720
uses movement artistry to
inspire and change the world

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.170
while elevating the artistic,
educational, and social

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:52.470
impact of dance.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.620
And I can't take credit for
the strength of that statement,

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:56.050
although I really
want to, like--

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:57.425
like, really, really bad.

00:08:57.425 --> 00:09:00.140
MAI and its purpose
are the brain children

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:04.490
of two amazing movement
artists, Jon Boogz from Miami

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:06.650
and Lil Buck from Memphis.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:08.420
These fellas were nice
enough to put together

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.380
a bespoke piece for us.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:14.510
So without further ado,
I present MAI, featuring

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.576
the poetry of Robin Sanders.

00:09:17.576 --> 00:09:20.006
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:20.006 --> 00:09:23.408
Your attention, please.

00:09:23.408 --> 00:09:24.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:26.760
They are the stories
that we tell.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.740
And so they dance.

00:10:03.413 --> 00:10:04.213
Breathe.

00:10:21.608 --> 00:10:22.602
On the path.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:50.290
And so they danced.

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:53.330
And so they danced
under moonlit skies that

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:56.310
heard their cries for freedom.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.560
And so they danced
boldly with the librettos

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:04.880
of liberation in their bodies
and the earth beneath them.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:09.320
And so they danced
freely on the path

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:12.170
paved by their
predecessors, waving

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:15.860
like the waters that carried
their ancestry, frequently

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:17.870
flying with no wings.

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:22.160
Air Force 1s and Js lifted
them amongst the greats.

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:25.500
They land as legends.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.640
Their feet gangsta
walking on the streets

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:31.840
that birth legacy,
electric boogaloos,

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:34.460
G style, and rock steady.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:40.070
The heroes lived and loved,
danced through time in hand

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:45.620
claps, finger snaps, toe
taps, buck jumps, CCs,

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:50.780
hits, and pops to the
syncopated beat of djembe drums,

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:54.590
now rebirthed in the
bassnectar of hip hop

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:59.090
through hardships and
museums, our moving monuments.

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.980
We-- we honor them.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.530
They are the stories
that we tell.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:07.950
Their lives blazingly
declare that we are here

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:14.550
from generation to generation,
gliding and spinning, history

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:17.020
bearing the image of greatness.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:19.335
We are now the new ancients.

00:13:30.572 --> 00:13:33.027
Your attention, please.

00:13:33.027 --> 00:13:36.464
[ELECTRICAL BUZZING]

00:13:43.829 --> 00:13:46.090
Oh, damn.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.400
Didn't see y'all
watching me robot.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:51.650
Couldn't help my little self.

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:53.570
Those fellas are
truly inspiring.

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:56.300
And Robin's poetry is ear candy.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:58.800
You can't get diabetes
in your ears, can you?

00:13:58.800 --> 00:13:59.600
Probably not, right?

00:14:03.086 --> 00:14:05.576
[WHISTLES]

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:10.556
[FLOOR SQUEAKING]

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:13.600
For our final piece,
I'd like to introduce

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.796
you all to Tomi Adeyemi.

00:14:15.796 --> 00:14:17.950
So to make sure I didn't
mess up her last name,

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:19.780
I made a little game for myself.

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:22.300
I just think of her being
the final ingredient

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:24.340
in a delicious literary stew.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:25.510
Like, I'll standing
over the pot,

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:28.000
and I just need to
add a yemi to it.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.430
Tomi is a "New York
Times" best-selling author

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:33.850
who's written two books and is
currently working on a third.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:36.230
I personally am a
fan for many reasons.

00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:38.800
One, my girl is from
Ashah, like Craig.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:42.010
Two, my girl left a safe
job to pursue her dream.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:44.550
I did the same when I left
teaching to pursue comedy.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.170
Three, she's a huge
"Harry Potter" fan.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:48.822
Me too, y'all.

00:14:48.822 --> 00:14:52.410
I wonder if Tomi me can
write the next "Harry Potter"

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:53.680
but make Harry a brother.

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:56.910
[GASPS] Maybe I'll pitch
that to her sometime.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:59.110
Now, let's all sit
back, relax, and enjoy.

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:00.580
Say it with me, y'all.

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.090
Tomi, Tomi, black girl magic.

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:07.390
Tomi, Tomi, black girl magic.

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:09.845
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:15:09.845 --> 00:15:13.773
Your attention, please.

00:15:13.773 --> 00:15:17.210
[TYPING]

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:26.840
What is magic?

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:30.291
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:15:30.291 --> 00:15:35.050
A boy casting a
spell with a wand?

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:39.340
A fairy godmother turning
pumpkins into carriages?

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.650
For this magi, magic is words.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:49.740
It's words that can
open minds, words

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:56.010
that can lift souls, words that
can change the world forever.

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:59.287
But a lot has to happen
before that magic can work.

00:15:59.287 --> 00:16:00.718
[SIGHS]

00:16:03.110 --> 00:16:04.580
Each act is a battle.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.095
Every word, an attack.

00:16:12.710 --> 00:16:14.150
Every story, a war.

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.410
To cast this magic,
the young magi must

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:27.470
first gather her inspiration.

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:32.300
She must take the seeds of a
story buried deep in her mind,

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:34.175
and make it come
alive before her eyes.

00:16:41.543 --> 00:16:43.010
It's like, I don't even
know if this section

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:44.722
should be in the book.

00:16:44.722 --> 00:16:47.130
[DOG WHIMPERING]

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:48.810
I don't think she would do that.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:50.640
I don't feel like that
feels true to her character.

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:51.870
[DOG BARKS]

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:52.890
It doesn't feel right.

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.470
I appreciate the
suggestion, but--

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:57.990
like, I get what you're
saying, but that doesn't

00:16:57.990 --> 00:16:59.790
change who she is as a person.

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:01.116
[DOG BARKS]

00:17:01.116 --> 00:17:02.520
OK, OK.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:03.320
You can go.

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:05.300
Thank you.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.250
Once all the magi's
ideas are out,

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.980
she must put them together
to create her magic.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:14.240
She must consider what character
arcs are most dynamic, what

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:18.430
plot points are
most exciting, what

00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:23.980
worldbuilding is most powerful,
what themes she must reflect.

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:26.050
She must refine the
bones of the story

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:28.630
until she has something
that makes sense.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:32.234
Then, she must go to work.

00:17:32.234 --> 00:17:33.060
[SIGHS]

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:39.430
The magi must write again
and again, never stopping--

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:40.410
[SIGHS]

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:41.240
--never letting up.

00:17:44.108 --> 00:17:46.020
[SIGHS]

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:52.058
She must create hundreds
of pages, burn those pages,

00:17:52.058 --> 00:17:56.420
and then write again.

00:17:56.420 --> 00:18:01.310
She must fight the words until
the magic can come alive.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:06.210
But when the magi's work is
done, her magic is powerful.

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.490
It spreads around the world.

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.270
It takes root in
heart after heart.

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:13.504
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:13.504 --> 00:18:24.590
It empowers those who
feel its rush, calling

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:27.650
them to cast their own magic.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:42.550
And it lives forever, because
powerful magic never dies.

00:18:49.216 --> 00:18:51.680
Your attention, please.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:54.350
Well, that about does it
for today's episode, y'all.

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:57.890
Damn, that was fun,
educational, and eye-opening.

00:18:57.890 --> 00:18:59.690
I hope you feel the same.

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:01.570
Don't forget to tune
into next week's episode,

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:04.225
where we'll do it again
with three new friends.

00:19:04.225 --> 00:19:06.470
Now I'm going to get out of
here and hit the library,

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:08.330
then work on my
dance moves, and then

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:09.990
put some hours in on my next--

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:11.930
well, first novel.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:13.580
I mean, I've written
children's books,

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.500
but I've got to get some
longer-form intellectual

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:17.720
property popping in 2020.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:19.760
Don't forget to listen to
the podcast, where we dive

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.900
even deeper into our stories.

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:23.460
Check it out on
iHeart, or wherever

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.230
you get your podcast fix.

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:28.560
And as always, don't be
afraid to find what you love.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.050
Share it with the world.

00:19:30.050 --> 00:19:34.020
And scream from the mountain
top, your attention, please!

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:37.513
[ELECTRICAL BUZZING]

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:05.156
And cut.
Cut, cut.

00:20:05.156 --> 00:20:05.956
There we go.

00:20:05.956 --> 00:20:06.954
Cut.

00:20:06.954 --> 00:20:10.447
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:12.443 --> 00:20:13.490
[CLAPPERBOARD CLAPS]

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:15.820
The boy is bad.

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:17.740
And that's bad meaning good,
for those who didn't get it.

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:22.930
I'm not going to do a
popcorn take, because I'm

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.700
not going to eat it.

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.480
Because if I eat it, then
I'll be thinking about it

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:29.085
and chewing on it,
stuck in my teeth.

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:40.570
Even if it is
something that will

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:43.570
be hard to explain to your
uncle at the family reunion--

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:45.058
[CHUCKLES]

00:21:04.898 --> 00:21:05.890
[LAUGHS]

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:08.866
[CHEERING]

00:21:14.818 --> 00:21:16.306
[GROANS]

00:21:16.306 --> 00:21:17.298
What the heck.

00:21:17.298 --> 00:21:18.098
[LAUGHS]

00:21:18.098 --> 00:21:19.778
Come on, let's do it again.

00:21:32.178 --> 00:21:35.600
Hey, how was the
robot, by the way?

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:37.520
[INAUDIBLE]

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:41.720
[LAUGHS] What's up, y'all?

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:43.880
It's me, Craig Robinson.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.130
Now, listen.

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:47.880
Head over to Hulu
and watch the four

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:52.780
episodes of "Your Attention
Please," hosted by yours truly.

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:54.620
I promise your little
minds will be blown when

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:56.150
you see what we cooked up--

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:59.060
amazing stories about powerful
new heroes and black culture

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:01.760
out there killing it
in spaces and places

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.230
you won't even see coming.

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:04.700
So go check it out and
let us know what you

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:06.200
think in the comments section.

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:07.250
I'll be waiting.

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:08.780
And as always,
don't forget to like

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:10.580
and subscribe to this channel.

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:12.130
Later.

